# Mutation plan for the seven routes of `api4/channel_join_request.go`.
#
#   scripts/go-discoverable.sh start
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/channel-join-requests.plan
#
# **The discoverable oracle must be running.** Six of the seven parity tests assert nothing without
# it, and `parity/channel_join_requests.rs` panics rather than skipping for exactly that reason —
# `scripts/stack.sh up` starts it now.
#
# The decisions these cover: the list's `Id DESC` tiebreak (two seeded rows share a `CreateAt`, so
# dropping it reorders the answer) and its direction; the offset being `page * per_page`; the two
# pagination clamps, which exist **twice** — once in the app layer and once in the store — with
# different floors; the status filter's default and the two count queries' separate predicates; the
# partial unique index being matched by *name*; `PreSave`/`PreUpdate` running at the store rather
# than at the caller; the seven guard refusals and their order and status codes; the review
# accepting only two of the four statuses; both places that drop the requester's free text; the
# conflict path answering 201 with the existing row instead of 409; the bodiless 404; the trailing
# newline; the `edit_other_users` gate being a string comparison; and the feature flag itself.
#
# A `\n` in a pattern becomes a newline, so no anchor here can quote Rust's own `b'\n'` — which is
# why `ENCODER_NEWLINE` is a named constant in `channel_join_requests.rs`.
#
# # Two decisions this plan deliberately does not mutate, because nothing could catch them
#
# * **The patch's `invalid_body_param` parameter name.** `requestJoinChannel` names `body` and
#   `patchChannelJoinRequest` names `channel_join_request_patch`; Go interpolates the name into
#   `message`, but until i18n lands ours *is* the raw id ([D-092]) and the name reaches the wire
#   nowhere else — `AppError.params` is not serialised. Swapping the two is invisible by
#   construction, not for want of a fixture.
# * **`UpdateChannelJoinRequest`'s cross-channel 404.** Reaching it needs an admin of **two**
#   channels reviewing one channel's request against the other, because the handler's permission
#   check fires first for anyone else. Worth a fixture; it is not here.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.
#
# # The tally (2026-09-12)
#
# **41 run, 38 caught, 3 survived, 0 harness faults — both controls SURVIVED**, so the batch is
# valid. Two of the three survivors are those controls; the third is an equivalent mutant. Two
# lines survived the *first* run and are counted above only after the fixtures that could not see
# them were fixed and each was re-run alone:
#
# * `store-list-drops-the-id-tiebreak` — the tied pair was planted **high id first**. With no
#   tiebreak the query is a seq scan and a sort, and Postgres's sort is stable at this size, so the
#   tied rows came back in insertion order — which was the same answer `Id DESC` gives. The right
#   answer and the wrong answer coincided. Planting `mmm` before `zzz` separates them; CAUGHT.
#   Measured against Postgres directly rather than reasoned about.
# * `app-a-negative-page-survives` — the predicate is `page < 0` and the mutation loosens it to
#   `< -1`, which `-3` cannot see. The test now drives `-1` first, and `-1` is the only value that
#   separates the two; CAUGHT.
#
# The equivalent mutant is `app-review-keeps-a-stale-denial-reason`: removing
# `current.denial_reason = String::new()` before the re-set is invisible **by construction**,
# because the row reaching that line is always `pending` and `IsValid` refuses a non-empty
# `DenialReason` on any status but `denied`. So a pending row can never carry one to clear. The
# assignment stays — it is Go's, and it is the guard that makes the re-set safe if the reachable
# set ever widens — but no fixture can catch its removal.
#

